I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.” The two reply texts are as follows, with \n denoting a newline. First: ```json\n{"a":1} {"b":2}\n```\n{"c":3}. Second: {"c":3}. For the first, the rule requires the answer {"b":2}, because the code fence holds two objects and the last one inside the fence is {"b":2}. For the second, the rule requires {"c":3}, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is {"c":3}. The check recover_json_object returns {"c":3} for both: on the first, the fenced pool tries to parse the whole fence content as one JSON object and fails, so it falls through to the top-level pool and returns the last top-level object {"c":3}; on the second it returns {"c":3} directly. Thus the check gives the same answer where the rule requires different answers. I would be wrong if recover_json_object returned {"b":2} for the first text, or if the documented rule were read as treating the entire fence content as a single candidate so that the first text has no scoreable fenced object and the top-level fallback is correct.